  <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/stock-market-crash-of-1929> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "Black Tuesday" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/stock-market-crash-of-1929> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "Crash of 1929" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/stock-market-crash-of-1929> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "Great Crash" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/stock-market-crash-of-1929> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "Wall Street Crash of 1929" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/stock-market-crash-of-1929> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader> <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/economic-history-of-the-united-states> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/stock-market-crash-of-1929> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The collapse of prices on the New York Stock Exchange in late October and November 1929, centred on Black Thursday (October 24) and Black Monday and Black Tuesday (October 28–29), which ended the speculative boom of the 1920s, wiped out about half the market's value within weeks and became the best-known symbol of the onset of the Great Depression." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/stock-market-crash-of-1929> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/scheme/economy-and-work> .
