  <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/muckrakers> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "Muckraker movement" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/muckrakers> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "Muckraking journalism" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/muckrakers> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#altLabel> "Muckraking" .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/muckrakers> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader> <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/investigative-journalism> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/muckrakers> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#broader> <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/progressive-movement> .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/muckrakers> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#definition> "The investigative journalists of the early 1900s, writing mainly in cheap mass-circulation magazines such as McClure's and Cosmopolitan, who exposed monopolies, political corruption, dangerous work and adulterated food, helping spark Progressive Era reforms; Theodore Roosevelt gave them their name in 1906 as a rebuke." .
  <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/muckrakers> <http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#inScheme> <https://github.com/john1hsu/usa-graph/id/scheme/gilded-age-and-progressive-era> .
